chunk_findings.detail:
`_hook_runs_pins` decides a hand-written hook will "re-record the pins" purely by substring presence — `"pins regenerate --after-merge" in text` — and never checks that the hook actually invokes it on the merge path. Any executable hook that merely *mentions* the command counts as installed, so `hook_notes` returns no reason and the "pin files are not re-recorded after a git merge" note is suppressed (and, for the merge path that consumes `hook_notes`, the merge is allowed to proceed) even though nothing will be regenerated. Concretely, a user's post-merge hook such as

```sh
#!/bin/sh
case "$(git diff --name-only ORIG_HEAD..HEAD)" in
  docs/*) ddflow pins regenerate --after-merge ;;
  *) : ;;
esac
```

or one where the command appears only as a comment (`# ddflow pins regenerate --after-merge`, e.g. disabled while bisecting) is executable and contains the substring, so `_hook_runs_pins` returns True. A merge touching only `ddflow/knobs.py`/another branch's regenerated README then leaves the knob table stale at 312 vs 313 with the doctor silent — exactly the B09da3e0b13 failure mode this change claims to close. The old check (HOOK_MARK only) never trusted foreign hooks, so this is a new false-negative introduced by widening the acceptance to a raw substring match; matching the shell line (e.g. a line whose command word is the regeneration, or running the hook in `--check` mode) would be required to claim it "calls" the command as the docstring states.

